{
  /*
    Agrippa's characters of the planetary names, and whether each is the
    name traced on its planet's square (plan 093, K6). One row a figure:
    the sixteen names Agrippa draws a character for, as his 1533 printing
    draws them and as Regardie's of 1940 redraws them, keyed by the name's
    id and the witness's. The trace is aiqBekar.ts's `traceHebrew`, the
    Golden Dawn's reduction; the names, their spellings and the witnesses
    are planetaryNames.json5's, beside this file.

    The figures. `leaf` is the archive.org leaf, `n164`, and `region` the
    figure's box, { x, y, width, height } in pixels, in archive.org's
    full-size image of that leaf,
    https://archive.org/download/<item>/page/<leaf>.jpg:
      agrippa1533: Agrippa, De occulta philosophia libri tres (Cologne,
        1533), Book II, chapter 22, item DeOccultaPhilosophiaLoc1533,
        n164–n168. Public domain.
      regardie1940: Regardie, The Golden Dawn, vol. 4 (Chicago, 1940),
        the plates of pp. 81–86, item IsraelRegardie-TheGoldenDawn-Vol4-1940,
        each name on its planet's leaf, n75–n80, as planetaryNames.json5
        gives it. In copyright where the site is read (plan 091): the
        verdicts and the boxes, and our words about the figures, only.

    How they were read, on 20 November 2026:
      1. Each figure was cropped from its page image and given an opaque
         id, so that nothing a reader saw named it.
      2. Two readers, Claude Opus 5.5 (reasoning high) and Claude Fable
         5.2 (reasoning high), working apart and blind to the names,
         recorded each figure's strokes, its vertices in order and the
         marks at its ends.
      3. Two judges, the same two models, working apart, compared each
         figure, with both readings, against the trace of every spelling
         the witnesses give for its name, with the final letters at their
         ordinary values and at 500 to 900, and gave a verdict, the
         nearest trace and where the figure departs from it.
      4. Claude (Opus 5.5) reconciled the two. Where the judges differ,
         on 7 figures, `note` says how it was settled; both verdicts are
         kept in `judges`.
    The working files (the crops, the comparison sheets, the readings of
    the 1940 figures and the judges' notes) are kept outside the
    repository, with the owner.

    A row holds:
      planetaryNameId  the name whose character it is.
      witness, leaf, region  where the figure is, as above.
      verdict     match: the spelling's trace, drawn freely. Scale,
                    rotation and mirroring are free; a return may be a U,
                    a wedge or two parallel legs; two letters on one cell
                    may be a notch or a cap.
                  partial: the trace, with the departures named.
                  no: not recognisably any spelling's trace.
      spelling    the Hebrew whose trace it is, or the nearest: a
                  witness's `he` in planetaryNames.json5, or the name's
                  `division`.
      finals      how the trace counts final letters: `ordinary` (and any
                  spelling with none), `final` (500 to 900), or `mixed`,
                  some words each way, which `cellsRead` shows.
      cellsRead   a partial's only: the cells the figure's vertices sit
                  on, word by word, which are not the trace's.
      departures  where the figure leaves the trace, in our words; none
                  for a match.
      marks       the marks at its ends and turns, in our words.
      judges      each judge's own verdict, { opus, fable }.
      note        where the judges differ, how it was reconciled.
      readings    the 1533 figures only: each reader's record, { opus,
                  fable }, a list of strokes, each { closed, vertices }.
                  A vertex is { kind, x, y, mark }, in pixels from the
                  region's top left corner. `kind`: end; corner; hairpin,
                  a rounded U, at its far point; cusp, a notch, cap or
                  kink; curve, an end of an arc. `mark`, at an end only:
                  circle, dot, bar, hook, none or other. Regardie's
                  figures are in copyright, and their readings are not
                  here.

    What the rows show. The 1940 figures keep 1533's paths and their
    departures, and differ only in the drawing: Kedemel's start is moved,
    and Iophiel's and Hagiel's capped turn sits on cell 10, where 1533's
    is nearer 11. The departures recur: ר on cell 2, not 20, in Graphiel,
    Bartzabel and Sorath; ל on 3, not 30, in Nakhiel and Hagiel; י and א
    drawn as one capped turn in Iophiel, Hagiel and Tiriel; a letter with
    no vertex in Chasmodai and Graphiel. So they are the characters' own,
    not one reader's slips.
   */

